    "convergence_verdict": {
      "fixed_alpha_convergence": "falsified",
      "statement": "At fixed alpha = 0.10 the error against Martin & Moyce (1952) is 17.1 % / 10.6 % / 12.5 % / 16.4 % at dx = 0.04 / 0.03 / 0.02 / 0.01 m: the sequence is non-monotone, the minimum sits at dx = 0.03 m and the finest level (dx = 0.01 m, MAE 0.2801) is worse than the coarsest. Only 1 of 4 levels meet both gates.",
      "mechanism": "the per-application shift is resolution independent (|dr| = alpha h^2 |grad-1|, h ~ dx, |grad-1| ~ 1/dx), while a fixed physical duration contains N = T/dt ~ 1/dx applications, so the cumulative regularisation rate ~ alpha dx weakens under refinement; the optimum therefore drifts up (0.10 at dx = 0.03 -> 0.15 at dx = 0.01)",
      "may_be_quoted_as": "a calibrated point at dx = 0.03 m (MAE 0.1423 Z/a, 10.6 % of the experiment), NOT as a converged limit",
